      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{60ee9e81572cc51ad4e847e6|full_name} \n\nPrimarily, it improves the readability of text and legibility of images. It also heavily reduces shimmering artifacts, which are a huge visual issue in VR. And it does this without needing world authors to go to extra lengths to ensure it. It's a fantastic thing. \n\nIn VR, any images being displayed will not naturally confirm to the pixel layout of the VR headset's display due to the lens distortions required to correct for it. This means any aliasing artifacts caused by the texture sampling mode on the source textures is going to be heavily amplified in a VR headset. As UI textures don't support mipmapping properly, they would suffer from tremendous shimmering at a distance without this. \n\nThe shader is included in the SDK for reference, as \"VRChat-Mobile-SupersampledUnlit.shader\" or  \"VRChat/Mobile/Worlds/Supersampled UI\" in Unity. What you see on screen is essentially the same as what would you would get if you rendered the canvas at a higher resolution and scaled it down. \n\nPreviously, it was possible to mitigate these artifacts using a custom UI shader, but this required extra setup from the world creator side. \n\nNote that this is specifically a shader thing... **not** a change to the texture filtering mode. This only affects UI elements using the default shader. If you use a non-default shader, it will not be affected.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
